WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.492 --> 00:00:11.950
CHRISTOPHER CROSBIE:
Hey, everyone.

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:14.220
I'm excited to be here to
discuss how you can speed up

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:16.890
your software development with
better insights and deeper

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:19.110
diagnostics with new
capabilities that

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:22.080
have been jointly developed from
the BigQuery and Cloud Logging

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:24.570
teams.

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:25.830
I'm Christopher Crosbie.

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:28.440
I'm a product manager
focused on advanced analytics

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:29.640
here at Google Cloud.

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:32.400
Later I'll be joined by Charles
from the Cloud Logging team.

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:36.990
So a fundamental
requirement for enterprises

00:00:36.990 --> 00:00:39.630
wanting to transform
themselves digitally

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:42.540
is a need to store,
manage, and analyze

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:47.130
large quantities of operational
data from a variety of sources.

00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:50.550
Logs are a critical data
source for troubleshooting

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:53.130
machine learning, data
analytics, and security.

00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:54.900
So while Cloud Logging
today can already

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:59.760
handle massive amounts of data,
capturing petabytes with ease,

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:03.750
many developers still struggle
to analyze this log data,

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:06.720
and they're unable to find
those symptoms in the log that

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:08.340
lead to deeper diagnostics.

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:11.010
And despite the large
volumes of data,

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:13.920
often they still find it hard
to move from a retrospective log

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:17.310
analysis to predictive
insights and prevent issues

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:18.880
before they happen.

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:22.500
So to help you achieve this next
level of software development

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:25.140
with better insight
and deeper diagnostics,

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:27.300
Charles and I are
thrilled to announce

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:30.810
Log Analytics, a new
comprehensive, easy to use,

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:32.850
cost effective,
and performant tool

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:36.390
within Cloud Logging that
has the performance and power

00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:38.560
of BigQuery behind it.

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:40.810
This capability should
help you use your logging

00:01:40.810 --> 00:01:42.970
data for both
better diagnostics,

00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:47.250
as well as getting more
insight into your business.

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:49.170
Now with the companies
I've worked with,

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:52.530
I've seen several teams
that rely on logging data.

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:55.320
Yet the majority
of the users really

00:01:55.320 --> 00:02:00.510
struggle to analyze that high
volume of messy log data.

00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:02.760
And that leaves
organizations with a lot

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:05.030
of unrealized potential.

00:02:05.030 --> 00:02:07.210
So the new Log Analytics
feature is directly

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:10.780
targeted at those teams who are
wrestling to extract insight

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:14.440
that they need from that hard
to analyze logging information.

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:17.710
This is targeted at DevOps
teams who want to improve system

00:02:17.710 --> 00:02:20.870
reliability, and they might be
struggling with analyzing logs

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:25.960
at large scale and want a
centralized logging repository.

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:28.990
Or IT teams that need insight
into how to better manage

00:02:28.990 --> 00:02:30.640
their fleet, and
they're struggling

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:33.550
to analyze signals across
multiple clouds or even data

00:02:33.550 --> 00:02:36.520
centers and look at the
trends over historical amounts

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:38.110
of time.

00:02:38.110 --> 00:02:40.180
Security teams that
want to identify

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:43.150
attacks, and they want to start
taking advantage of the latest

00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:44.920
and greatest machine
learning and advanced

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:48.670
analysis techniques to
detect those threats.

00:02:48.670 --> 00:02:52.690
Or business users that want to
make better decisions but today

00:02:52.690 --> 00:02:54.460
they struggle to
get logs integrated

00:02:54.460 --> 00:02:56.770
with their visualization
and analytic tools,

00:02:56.770 --> 00:02:59.710
and they struggle to combine
their logs data and business

00:02:59.710 --> 00:03:01.237
data.

00:03:01.237 --> 00:03:02.820
Now when I go and
talk to these teams,

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:05.970
it becomes pretty obvious what
the source of their struggle

00:03:05.970 --> 00:03:07.740
is.

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:11.220
What makes log
analytics hard today

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:13.350
is the complexity
of the data systems

00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:16.153
that organizations need to run.

00:03:16.153 --> 00:03:17.820
So most of you watching,
you're probably

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:20.040
familiar with a similar
convoluted architecture

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:21.000
at your own company.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:24.750
Where possibly application logs,
they go into an elastic index,

00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:27.120
and then that's where your
application performance

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:29.550
monitoring or Kibana
dashboards are.

00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:31.470
But then you have
lower level operating

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:34.680
system network logs that flow
into a proprietary security

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:36.030
vendor's database.

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:37.770
Then of course,
there's other machine

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:41.310
generated logs, like maybe
from sensors or IoT data.

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:43.980
And they get stored
in a format that's

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:46.290
optimized for time
series analysis, which

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:49.600
is then downsampled
into a data warehouse.

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:52.350
And so there is a lot of
overhead with all of this,

00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:55.530
and you definitely
pay an additional tax

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:59.070
for extracting and copying
all that data around.

00:03:59.070 --> 00:04:01.460
So when I chat with the
end users of these systems,

00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:05.210
they tell me that today's
common architecture patterns,

00:04:05.210 --> 00:04:07.820
it generates five
critical issues

00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:11.450
that make this log
analytics so hard.

00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:14.540
First is the latency issues.

00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:16.279
There's just a lot
of additional hops,

00:04:16.279 --> 00:04:19.010
and that means data takes
longer to get to different end

00:04:19.010 --> 00:04:20.450
systems.

00:04:20.450 --> 00:04:23.600
Two, if no clear
source of truth.

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:26.115
Data transformation might
happen in multiple places.

00:04:26.115 --> 00:04:27.740
That makes it a
challenge to understand

00:04:27.740 --> 00:04:29.900
what's happened to the data.

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:32.210
Three, moving to
machine learning

00:04:32.210 --> 00:04:34.520
requires a lot of
additional hoops.

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:37.790
It is not trivial to just take
a TensorFlow model or a notebook

00:04:37.790 --> 00:04:41.100
and put that on top of
these various systems.

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:44.330
Four, the reliance on
multiple interfaces

00:04:44.330 --> 00:04:47.270
where the same users need
to learn different systems

00:04:47.270 --> 00:04:50.300
just has a whole mess of
different applications and tabs

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:54.320
that users have to manually
reconcile themselves.

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:56.340
And even with all
this user pain,

00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:58.310
the IT teams still
have to manage

00:04:58.310 --> 00:05:00.470
a lot of high cost
and management

00:05:00.470 --> 00:05:03.050
complexities of running
all these different vendor

00:05:03.050 --> 00:05:06.150
databases and applications.

00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:07.980
When I ask customers
what they want,

00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:09.540
it essentially
boils down to having

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:13.800
a separation between the
log aggregation and the log

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:15.540
applications.

00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:17.580
More of our tech savvy
customers have already

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:20.160
started to adopt BigQuery
as their centralized log

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:23.670
aggregation store for data
collection, normalization,

00:05:23.670 --> 00:05:27.910
and storage management tasks
like auditing and compliance.

00:05:27.910 --> 00:05:31.350
Now here at Google, it
does seem intuitive to us

00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:33.180
that customers are
choosing BigQuery

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:36.760
as their next generation
logging analysis platform.

00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:38.650
Dremel, which is
the query engine

00:05:38.650 --> 00:05:41.500
that BigQuery is based
on, that was originally

00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:44.050
designed and built for logs.

00:05:44.050 --> 00:05:45.820
And it's being used
for over a decade

00:05:45.820 --> 00:05:49.180
for analyzing logs and creating
sophisticated machine learning

00:05:49.180 --> 00:05:52.420
on those logs for some of
Google's biggest applications--

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:55.300
Search, YouTube, Gmail.

00:05:55.300 --> 00:05:57.250
And so if I start
to compare BigQuery

00:05:57.250 --> 00:05:59.110
against typical
logging platforms

00:05:59.110 --> 00:06:02.080
that customers are using today
there's some clear advantages

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:03.730
that I'm seeing.

00:06:03.730 --> 00:06:05.830
First is just the management.

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:08.500
Today's logging platforms
need more automation

00:06:08.500 --> 00:06:11.740
just due to the applications
shifting into ephemeral servers

00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:13.840
and microservice architectures.

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:16.000
It's just becoming way
too hard to keep up

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:17.650
with all the various
IT management

00:06:17.650 --> 00:06:20.440
tasks that are required for
running the various logging

00:06:20.440 --> 00:06:21.410
backends.

00:06:21.410 --> 00:06:24.810
BigQuery, fully
managed, serverless.

00:06:24.810 --> 00:06:28.590
Two, customers need
data lake scalability.

00:06:28.590 --> 00:06:31.470
Logs are coming
from all over now.

00:06:31.470 --> 00:06:34.320
On prem, multiple clouds,
Kubernetes, real world IoT

00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:34.860
systems.

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:37.260
What used to really
be a telco scale

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:39.390
for a lot of these
security vendors

00:06:39.390 --> 00:06:42.480
that relied on logs
data is pretty standard

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:44.130
for your average enterprise now.

00:06:44.130 --> 00:06:46.950
And when it comes to
security analysis,

00:06:46.950 --> 00:06:50.040
you need these logs in
a centralized place.

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:52.110
Threats are not system specific.

00:06:52.110 --> 00:06:54.150
A hacker got into GCP?

00:06:54.150 --> 00:06:56.580
Guess what, they probably
got into AWS, too.

00:06:56.580 --> 00:07:00.492
And you want to review and
track that in one location.

00:07:00.492 --> 00:07:01.950
It's equally
important to also have

00:07:01.950 --> 00:07:03.540
a full history of the logs.

00:07:03.540 --> 00:07:05.130
That's becoming more important.

00:07:05.130 --> 00:07:08.990
If you look at something like
the recent SolarWinds attack,

00:07:08.990 --> 00:07:11.540
that went back over 9 months.

00:07:11.540 --> 00:07:13.040
It doesn't work
anymore to just have

00:07:13.040 --> 00:07:16.640
30 or 60 days worth of logs
in your security database.

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:19.190
You need something that can
cost effectively, independently

00:07:19.190 --> 00:07:23.450
scale the storage and
analysis like BigQuery can.

00:07:23.450 --> 00:07:27.590
And speaking of security,
the security posture

00:07:27.590 --> 00:07:30.140
that many of these legacy
or on prem log systems

00:07:30.140 --> 00:07:33.560
had relied on security
controls like maybe the network

00:07:33.560 --> 00:07:36.240
as a primary layer
of protection.

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:37.790
But now in our
cross cloud world,

00:07:37.790 --> 00:07:40.850
things like network
perimeters are vanishing.

00:07:40.850 --> 00:07:44.135
And you mix that with tougher
regulations like GDPR,

00:07:44.135 --> 00:07:46.220
you really need a
backend now that

00:07:46.220 --> 00:07:48.680
can help you achieve a
security posture that

00:07:48.680 --> 00:07:52.170
makes it easy to align
for our digitalized world

00:07:52.170 --> 00:07:56.010
and that you can trust with
your logging information.

00:07:56.010 --> 00:07:58.260
Another big one that I'm
really hearing from customers

00:07:58.260 --> 00:08:01.440
is a need for advanced
analytics and custom ML.

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:04.530
Customers are finding that these
pre-canned outlier dashboards

00:08:04.530 --> 00:08:07.980
that their logs applications are
coming with just don't cut it

00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:09.348
for the systems today.

00:08:09.348 --> 00:08:10.890
I was working with
one enterprise who

00:08:10.890 --> 00:08:13.440
moved over their existing
[INAUDIBLE] to GKE

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:17.442
and almost instantly got
30,000 erroneous alerts.

00:08:17.442 --> 00:08:18.900
So customers have
told me that they

00:08:18.900 --> 00:08:21.750
want to do their own analysis,
develop their own algorithms

00:08:21.750 --> 00:08:25.150
that are designed for their
architecture, their business.

00:08:25.150 --> 00:08:27.970
BigQuery is already being used
by some of the largest security

00:08:27.970 --> 00:08:31.570
vendors out there to develop
XDR platforms as an alternative

00:08:31.570 --> 00:08:33.520
to reactive approaches
of the [INAUDIBLE]..

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:36.010
And they're doing that
with GCP AI capabilities

00:08:36.010 --> 00:08:39.650
like BQML and Vertex.

00:08:39.650 --> 00:08:42.500
Finally, there's just
a lot of new use cases

00:08:42.500 --> 00:08:46.640
that customers want to use,
that customers are realizing.

00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:48.920
Customers, they understand
now that there's

00:08:48.920 --> 00:08:51.740
a lot of business value in
joining all of this logging

00:08:51.740 --> 00:08:53.600
data to their business
data, and then they

00:08:53.600 --> 00:08:55.097
want to share it with others.

00:08:55.097 --> 00:08:56.930
I'm working with an
e-commerce company who's

00:08:56.930 --> 00:09:00.170
analyzing weblog data alongside
their clickstream data

00:09:00.170 --> 00:09:03.020
to better understand how users
interact with their site,

00:09:03.020 --> 00:09:05.540
and then they can offer them
better product recommendations.

00:09:05.540 --> 00:09:08.520
And that's already led to
increased sales for them.

00:09:08.520 --> 00:09:11.240
So having logs that can be
easily analyzed and joined

00:09:11.240 --> 00:09:13.550
the rest of your warehouse,
that just opens up

00:09:13.550 --> 00:09:16.220
a whole new set of use cases
like predicting customer

00:09:16.220 --> 00:09:18.380
behavior, optimizing
online marketing campaigns,

00:09:18.380 --> 00:09:20.300
and maybe even
understanding how employees

00:09:20.300 --> 00:09:24.320
engage with different software
solutions in your organization.

00:09:24.320 --> 00:09:26.530
So to unlock these use
cases and help customers

00:09:26.530 --> 00:09:30.430
better understand the logs data
that they bring to BigQuery,

00:09:30.430 --> 00:09:32.500
we're enhancing support
for log analytics

00:09:32.500 --> 00:09:36.030
with investments in
three core areas.

00:09:36.030 --> 00:09:38.850
Semi-structured
data, search indexes,

00:09:38.850 --> 00:09:41.190
and time series analysis.

00:09:41.190 --> 00:09:43.050
These new features
and functions are

00:09:43.050 --> 00:09:45.150
going to let you
explore and analyze

00:09:45.150 --> 00:09:47.490
the wealth of logging
data that you have

00:09:47.490 --> 00:09:49.660
alongside your business data.

00:09:49.660 --> 00:09:52.230
So with native JSON, there's
going to be a new data type

00:09:52.230 --> 00:09:53.640
specific to JSON.

00:09:53.640 --> 00:09:56.190
And it's going to
on a load shred

00:09:56.190 --> 00:09:58.260
that JSON document that
can be unstructured,

00:09:58.260 --> 00:10:01.290
constantly changing, into
a columnar store format

00:10:01.290 --> 00:10:03.750
so you can get the same
performance and cost

00:10:03.750 --> 00:10:05.700
effectiveness that
you've come to expect

00:10:05.700 --> 00:10:08.800
from any other structured
data in BigQuery.

00:10:08.800 --> 00:10:11.980
Along with that, you're going to
find additional JSON functions,

00:10:11.980 --> 00:10:15.160
like a dot subscript notation
to make it a lot easier to work

00:10:15.160 --> 00:10:18.200
with these JSON data sets.

00:10:18.200 --> 00:10:20.420
Second, the search
indexes are going

00:10:20.420 --> 00:10:23.270
to provide text indexes that
provide users the ability

00:10:23.270 --> 00:10:25.940
to run what I call needle
in the haystack type

00:10:25.940 --> 00:10:28.330
queries on the log data.

00:10:28.330 --> 00:10:31.700
This is when you want to
pinpoint a very specific data

00:10:31.700 --> 00:10:32.310
element.

00:10:32.310 --> 00:10:33.685
So let's say I
want to find Chris

00:10:33.685 --> 00:10:35.870
Crosbie in a petabyte
of logging data

00:10:35.870 --> 00:10:38.275
so I can delete him
for GDPR reasons.

00:10:38.275 --> 00:10:39.650
This search index
is going to let

00:10:39.650 --> 00:10:42.020
you do that, or find
specific IP addresses

00:10:42.020 --> 00:10:46.630
or other specific data elements.

00:10:46.630 --> 00:10:49.870
Finally, coming soon
there's going to be also

00:10:49.870 --> 00:10:52.010
TimeSeries joins and data types.

00:10:52.010 --> 00:10:55.420
So we're going to support
joining table by arbitrary time

00:10:55.420 --> 00:10:57.640
windows, where we
sample that data,

00:10:57.640 --> 00:11:01.210
and you'll also be able to
store time periods, as opposed

00:11:01.210 --> 00:11:03.710
to just a point in time.

00:11:03.710 --> 00:11:06.833
So here at GCP we're putting
our money where our mouth is

00:11:06.833 --> 00:11:08.500
and we're standing
behind these features

00:11:08.500 --> 00:11:10.970
in our own logs applications.

00:11:10.970 --> 00:11:14.630
We are using these features as
a base for our log analytics

00:11:14.630 --> 00:11:16.880
application from Cloud
Logging, and so now I'm

00:11:16.880 --> 00:11:18.440
going to hand you
over to Charles

00:11:18.440 --> 00:11:21.355
to tell you more about that.

00:11:21.355 --> 00:11:22.480
CHARLES BAER: Thanks Chris.

00:11:22.480 --> 00:11:23.620
My name is Charles Baer.

00:11:23.620 --> 00:11:25.450
I'm a product manager
in Cloud Logging,

00:11:25.450 --> 00:11:28.470
and Chris and I have been
partnering on Log Analytics.

00:11:28.470 --> 00:11:32.110
Log Analytics combines the
purpose built logging platform

00:11:32.110 --> 00:11:35.200
provided by Cloud Logging
with BigQuery's powerful data

00:11:35.200 --> 00:11:36.610
analytics platform.

00:11:36.610 --> 00:11:38.980
And we build log analytics
and Cloud Logging

00:11:38.980 --> 00:11:40.630
for three reasons.

00:11:40.630 --> 00:11:43.060
First, ad hoc log analysis.

00:11:43.060 --> 00:11:45.820
To empower users to answer
sophisticated questions

00:11:45.820 --> 00:11:49.270
where ad hoc grouping can
deliver useful insights.

00:11:49.270 --> 00:11:52.270
For log aggregation-- to
answer the types of questions

00:11:52.270 --> 00:11:56.020
that require log aggregation
over large data volumes.

00:11:56.020 --> 00:11:57.880
And to build a
platform upon which

00:11:57.880 --> 00:12:02.320
both our users and our product
can grow for the future.

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:06.385
Now Cloud Logging is uniquely
positioned for log analytics.

00:12:06.385 --> 00:12:09.100
Cloud Logging provides an
integrated logging solution,

00:12:09.100 --> 00:12:11.650
both for services that
are running on top of GCP,

00:12:11.650 --> 00:12:14.380
as well as logs that are
generated externally.

00:12:14.380 --> 00:12:19.210
This includes log collection,
routing, storage, and analysis.

00:12:19.210 --> 00:12:22.050
All the things you need
in a logging platform.

00:12:22.050 --> 00:12:25.410
So in Cloud Logging, log
collection is really easy.

00:12:25.410 --> 00:12:27.300
Logs for services
on Google Cloud

00:12:27.300 --> 00:12:29.100
are automatically
collected, and it's

00:12:29.100 --> 00:12:31.380
easy to collect other
infrastructure and application

00:12:31.380 --> 00:12:32.880
logs if you need to.

00:12:32.880 --> 00:12:34.690
Getting the right logs is easy.

00:12:34.690 --> 00:12:39.530
It's a first critical step for
the logging analytics, as well.

00:12:39.530 --> 00:12:41.030
The second thing
is the logs router.

00:12:41.030 --> 00:12:44.260
It provides powerful
routing capabilities.

00:12:44.260 --> 00:12:48.010
The built in routing routes
logs to Cloud Logging, other GCP

00:12:48.010 --> 00:12:50.170
services like Cloud
Storage, and even

00:12:50.170 --> 00:12:52.570
to external third party
tools with integration

00:12:52.570 --> 00:12:53.980
through Pub/Sub.

00:12:53.980 --> 00:12:56.290
Now for log analytics
the platform routes

00:12:56.290 --> 00:12:58.870
logs to BigQuery.

00:12:58.870 --> 00:13:01.690
The storage platform provides
scalable log management

00:13:01.690 --> 00:13:02.980
capabilities.

00:13:02.980 --> 00:13:04.570
These capabilities
provide controls

00:13:04.570 --> 00:13:06.910
around what logs are stored,
where they're stored,

00:13:06.910 --> 00:13:09.490
how long they're stored, and
who can access them to meet

00:13:09.490 --> 00:13:11.820
your compliance requirements.

00:13:11.820 --> 00:13:15.740
For logging analysis, this
manages the storage and access

00:13:15.740 --> 00:13:17.790
in BigQuery.

00:13:17.790 --> 00:13:20.370
Cloud Logging already provides
several tools to analyze

00:13:20.370 --> 00:13:23.100
logs, each with
special purposes.

00:13:23.100 --> 00:13:25.770
The Logs Explorer is a tool
optimized for troubleshooting

00:13:25.770 --> 00:13:28.650
use cases with great
features like log streaming,

00:13:28.650 --> 00:13:32.490
a log Resource Explorer, and
a histogram for visualization.

00:13:32.490 --> 00:13:34.170
You have error
reporting, which helps

00:13:34.170 --> 00:13:36.660
users react to critical
application errors

00:13:36.660 --> 00:13:39.570
through automated error
grouping and notifications.

00:13:39.570 --> 00:13:42.570
And through log based metrics
dashboards and alerting,

00:13:42.570 --> 00:13:45.360
which all provide other
ways to understand and make

00:13:45.360 --> 00:13:48.660
your logs actionable for
operational use cases.

00:13:48.660 --> 00:13:51.480
Our new Log Analytics
feature expands this toolset

00:13:51.480 --> 00:13:54.990
to include ad hoc log
analysis capabilities.

00:13:54.990 --> 00:13:58.440
Taken together, Cloud Logging
provides a powerful platform

00:13:58.440 --> 00:14:02.940
specifically designed
for working with logs.

00:14:02.940 --> 00:14:05.820
So what is Log
Analytics exactly?

00:14:05.820 --> 00:14:08.070
Log analytics extends
the capabilities

00:14:08.070 --> 00:14:12.060
provided by Cloud Logging
in several important ways.

00:14:12.060 --> 00:14:14.400
First, log analytics
automatically

00:14:14.400 --> 00:14:17.940
stores logs in BigQuery with
a simple configuration setup.

00:14:17.940 --> 00:14:20.333
With a checkbox,
enable Log Analytics

00:14:20.333 --> 00:14:21.750
when you're creating
a log bucket,

00:14:21.750 --> 00:14:23.820
you can use Cloud
Logging's pipeline

00:14:23.820 --> 00:14:26.430
to make it easy to store
those logs, which you can then

00:14:26.430 --> 00:14:28.650
analyze in Log Analytics.

00:14:28.650 --> 00:14:31.530
Second, we're excited
for our new purpose built

00:14:31.530 --> 00:14:35.850
log analytics UI, which provides
access to the logs using SQL.

00:14:35.850 --> 00:14:38.790
SQL is a well known, widely
use domain specific language,

00:14:38.790 --> 00:14:40.800
has powerful aggregation
capabilities,

00:14:40.800 --> 00:14:43.740
and of course it's
widely used in BigQuery.

00:14:43.740 --> 00:14:46.470
When you need to group by,
or sum, or find a ratio,

00:14:46.470 --> 00:14:49.290
Log Analytics supports the
same standard SQL functions

00:14:49.290 --> 00:14:51.210
provided by BigQuery.

00:14:51.210 --> 00:14:53.880
Now the Log Analytics UI
provides an optimized results

00:14:53.880 --> 00:14:56.010
experience designed
specifically for working

00:14:56.010 --> 00:14:57.540
with unstructured log data.

00:14:57.540 --> 00:15:01.110
And you'll see more of this
experience in the demo later.

00:15:01.110 --> 00:15:04.350
Third, logs included in
Log Analytics are made

00:15:04.350 --> 00:15:05.790
available directly in BigQuery.

00:15:05.790 --> 00:15:08.850
If users need to join logs
with another data source,

00:15:08.850 --> 00:15:10.860
they can run the same
query in Log Analytics

00:15:10.860 --> 00:15:13.980
UI and the BigQuery UI and
join that data with data

00:15:13.980 --> 00:15:15.870
already in BigQuery.

00:15:15.870 --> 00:15:18.750
So by making this log data
available where users need it,

00:15:18.750 --> 00:15:21.195
Log Analytics can help
remove data silos.

00:15:27.090 --> 00:15:29.960
So how can you benefit from
these new capabilities?

00:15:29.960 --> 00:15:32.030
Log Analytics can
help reduce the need

00:15:32.030 --> 00:15:34.310
to send logs to so
many different systems

00:15:34.310 --> 00:15:36.650
to address the
separate use cases.

00:15:36.650 --> 00:15:39.300
I'll cover three common
use cases as examples.

00:15:39.300 --> 00:15:41.060
First is for the DevOps team.

00:15:41.060 --> 00:15:43.880
Resolving issues quickly is
critical to maintaining system

00:15:43.880 --> 00:15:47.270
reliability, especially
with user facing services.

00:15:47.270 --> 00:15:50.660
Log analytics helps here by
helping users more quickly find

00:15:50.660 --> 00:15:53.570
patterns by correlating
IDs across services

00:15:53.570 --> 00:15:56.870
so that you can reduce
outages more quickly.

00:15:56.870 --> 00:15:58.750
Faster troubleshooting
can also help improve

00:15:58.750 --> 00:16:00.125
the speed of
software development

00:16:00.125 --> 00:16:02.920
by reducing time spent on
debugging and therefore

00:16:02.920 --> 00:16:05.430
freeing up developer time.

00:16:05.430 --> 00:16:07.020
The second is security.

00:16:07.020 --> 00:16:08.460
Investigations are
a critical part

00:16:08.460 --> 00:16:11.100
of understanding and
remediating security threats.

00:16:11.100 --> 00:16:13.590
Log Analytics can help
here by finding logs

00:16:13.590 --> 00:16:18.240
across large time ranges, which
often means large data volumes.

00:16:18.240 --> 00:16:20.640
The third is IT operations.

00:16:20.640 --> 00:16:22.260
Managing a fleet of
different services

00:16:22.260 --> 00:16:24.690
is a core part of IT
operations, and it's

00:16:24.690 --> 00:16:27.000
important to understand
the events and trends

00:16:27.000 --> 00:16:28.710
across the whole fleet.

00:16:28.710 --> 00:16:30.570
Log Analytics helps
here by identifying

00:16:30.570 --> 00:16:34.360
patterns and the fleet
performance over time.

00:16:34.360 --> 00:16:36.110
Now there are many
ways that Log Analytics

00:16:36.110 --> 00:16:39.140
can help enable business
decisions, including analyzing

00:16:39.140 --> 00:16:41.180
business data reported in logs.

00:16:41.180 --> 00:16:43.340
And by unlocking
valuable insights,

00:16:43.340 --> 00:16:47.780
Log Analytics brings
new value to your logs.

00:16:47.780 --> 00:16:50.580
And now, since there's no better
way to show off a new feature,

00:16:50.580 --> 00:16:53.100
let's see a demo.

00:16:53.100 --> 00:16:56.390
In this demo, I'll show you
the new log analytics UI,

00:16:56.390 --> 00:16:58.370
an example of
combining data stored

00:16:58.370 --> 00:17:01.340
in Log Analytics with
other data in BigQuery.

00:17:01.340 --> 00:17:03.800
I'll highlight the capabilities
offered by Log Analytics

00:17:03.800 --> 00:17:05.430
to address two use cases.

00:17:05.430 --> 00:17:07.339
One is the DevOps
troubleshooting use case,

00:17:07.339 --> 00:17:11.140
and the other is a
security use case.

00:17:11.140 --> 00:17:13.880
For the demo I've instances
of the online boutique,

00:17:13.880 --> 00:17:16.480
which is an open source
microservices demo app.

00:17:16.480 --> 00:17:18.490
It's deployed to two
GK clusters, which

00:17:18.490 --> 00:17:20.410
are generating logs,
which are ingested

00:17:20.410 --> 00:17:24.250
into Cloud Logging using the
out of the box configuration.

00:17:24.250 --> 00:17:27.099
I've set up a logging
sink to capture the logs

00:17:27.099 --> 00:17:30.670
and send them to Log Analytics
Enable bucket in Cloud Logging.

00:17:30.670 --> 00:17:32.620
With a click of a
checkbox, logs are

00:17:32.620 --> 00:17:35.650
available both in Log
Analytics and Cloud Logging

00:17:35.650 --> 00:17:37.300
and in BigQuery.

00:17:37.300 --> 00:17:39.370
First let's look at
the Log Analytics

00:17:39.370 --> 00:17:42.910
UI, which is a new UI Cloud
Logging optimized for viewing

00:17:42.910 --> 00:17:44.020
logging data.

00:17:44.020 --> 00:17:46.870
In the Log Analytics UI,
use the same standard SQL

00:17:46.870 --> 00:17:48.340
that is available in BigQuery.

00:17:48.340 --> 00:17:50.740
SQL queries are used
to view the log data

00:17:50.740 --> 00:17:53.770
and can range from
simple or complex.

00:17:53.770 --> 00:17:55.540
There's a Query
Builder here where

00:17:55.540 --> 00:17:57.640
you can enter your SQL
in a results viewer

00:17:57.640 --> 00:17:59.320
to view the query results.

00:17:59.320 --> 00:18:02.950
To start, let's take a look
at the completed Kubernetes

00:18:02.950 --> 00:18:07.830
logs for the front end
service over the past hour.

00:18:07.830 --> 00:18:11.410
Once the query is valid
I can run the query.

00:18:11.410 --> 00:18:15.340
Now the Log
Analytics UI provides

00:18:15.340 --> 00:18:18.460
two specific optimizations that
have been added to BigQuery

00:18:18.460 --> 00:18:20.110
and used by Log Analytics.

00:18:20.110 --> 00:18:22.810
The first is that
JSON value function,

00:18:22.810 --> 00:18:25.510
which allows you to
extract JSON data

00:18:25.510 --> 00:18:29.080
and compare it to strings, which
is really useful for queries

00:18:29.080 --> 00:18:31.660
like this or when you
want to look at the string

00:18:31.660 --> 00:18:34.690
results of data stored in JSON.

00:18:34.690 --> 00:18:39.160
And the second optimisation
is the JSON dot notation.

00:18:39.160 --> 00:18:41.230
Notice that the JSON
payload dot message field

00:18:41.230 --> 00:18:43.150
is actually using
the dot notation

00:18:43.150 --> 00:18:47.020
to refer to a field inside
the JSON structure of a JSON

00:18:47.020 --> 00:18:47.890
payload.

00:18:47.890 --> 00:18:51.380
Both of these make it easier
to work with JSON data.

00:18:51.380 --> 00:18:54.650
The results are displayed
using a view optimized

00:18:54.650 --> 00:18:55.790
for working with log data.

00:18:55.790 --> 00:18:58.700
You can see that the
JSON payload is actually

00:18:58.700 --> 00:19:02.030
stored as JSON data, which
means that any JSON structured

00:19:02.030 --> 00:19:04.850
data can be stored in there
without having to know

00:19:04.850 --> 00:19:07.360
or specify the schema up front.

00:19:07.360 --> 00:19:10.100
Log Analytics UI, the
JSON data, is actually

00:19:10.100 --> 00:19:12.800
displayed using JSON
[INAUDIBLE] format

00:19:12.800 --> 00:19:16.110
to make it even easier to use.

00:19:16.110 --> 00:19:18.500
Now by looking at
some of these logs,

00:19:18.500 --> 00:19:22.520
I notice that there is a
value inside these logs, which

00:19:22.520 --> 00:19:25.650
is the response
took milliseconds,

00:19:25.650 --> 00:19:28.225
which is the time the
request took to complete.

00:19:28.225 --> 00:19:29.600
So one of the
things we can do is

00:19:29.600 --> 00:19:32.630
look at the min, max,
and average values

00:19:32.630 --> 00:19:34.840
of that over time.

00:19:34.840 --> 00:19:38.060
These kind of queries can be
very useful in troubleshooting.

00:19:38.060 --> 00:19:41.070
So let's take a look at
what this would look like.

00:19:41.070 --> 00:19:42.660
There we go.

00:19:42.660 --> 00:19:43.160
OK.

00:19:43.160 --> 00:19:46.340
So what we're doing here is
we're extracting this field,

00:19:46.340 --> 00:19:49.730
and we're casting it as an
integer so we can do the sum.

00:19:49.730 --> 00:19:50.855
We're also taking the hour.

00:19:50.855 --> 00:19:52.272
And then what we're
doing is we're

00:19:52.272 --> 00:19:54.200
grouping, looking at the
minimum, the maximum,

00:19:54.200 --> 00:19:57.390
and the average value.

00:19:57.390 --> 00:20:00.120
But what we notice by
looking at the results here

00:20:00.120 --> 00:20:03.930
is that around hour
14, both the maximum

00:20:03.930 --> 00:20:08.490
as well as the average time
increased substantially.

00:20:08.490 --> 00:20:11.160
So this gives me great insight
during troubleshooting,

00:20:11.160 --> 00:20:13.080
and it can help me
more quickly identify

00:20:13.080 --> 00:20:17.087
the signals, which can help me
resolve issues more quickly.

00:20:17.087 --> 00:20:18.920
Well, Log Analytics
provides the flexibility

00:20:18.920 --> 00:20:20.960
to look at individual
logs, as you see,

00:20:20.960 --> 00:20:23.240
and run complex
aggregations, s which

00:20:23.240 --> 00:20:26.210
can help surface critical
insights for the DevOps team

00:20:26.210 --> 00:20:27.990
when troubleshooting an issue.

00:20:27.990 --> 00:20:31.890
Now let's take a look at
the security use case.

00:20:31.890 --> 00:20:33.930
Again, staying with
the same scenario,

00:20:33.930 --> 00:20:35.877
the app deployed to GKE.

00:20:35.877 --> 00:20:37.710
This time let's look
at the security related

00:20:37.710 --> 00:20:40.170
logs for application
and infrastructure.

00:20:40.170 --> 00:20:42.757
We can do that by using
a query like this.

00:20:42.757 --> 00:20:44.340
The main difference
here is that we're

00:20:44.340 --> 00:20:47.230
looking at the proto
payload field here

00:20:47.230 --> 00:20:49.510
and we're looking
for audit logs.

00:20:49.510 --> 00:20:52.020
So why don't we run the query.

00:20:52.020 --> 00:20:55.380
Here's what the audit logs
look like in Log Analytics.

00:20:55.380 --> 00:20:59.760
You can see that they are the
record type within BigQuery,

00:20:59.760 --> 00:21:02.580
and you can see that there's a
detailed structure of the audit

00:21:02.580 --> 00:21:07.482
log within the
proto payload field.

00:21:07.482 --> 00:21:08.940
There's a lot of
information that's

00:21:08.940 --> 00:21:12.120
stored within an audit log,
and it's actually displayed

00:21:12.120 --> 00:21:14.700
in the UI using the
JSON [INAUDIBLE] format

00:21:14.700 --> 00:21:17.100
to make it easier to work with.

00:21:17.100 --> 00:21:19.860
All of the fields that are
actually stored as a record

00:21:19.860 --> 00:21:22.950
type are also displayed
using the JSON data type

00:21:22.950 --> 00:21:25.660
to make it easier to work with.

00:21:25.660 --> 00:21:29.650
Again, what's more interesting
is to look at the aggregations.

00:21:29.650 --> 00:21:31.240
So let's take a
look at the audit

00:21:31.240 --> 00:21:34.810
logs grouped by the
principal and the IP address

00:21:34.810 --> 00:21:37.270
fields, which are
the users as well

00:21:37.270 --> 00:21:43.210
as the IP address from which
those actions originated.

00:21:43.210 --> 00:21:43.720
Great.

00:21:43.720 --> 00:21:48.550
So now we can see both email
address and the IP address

00:21:48.550 --> 00:21:52.840
and the number of calls that
have originated from both.

00:21:52.840 --> 00:21:55.540
Now for security use
cases, sometimes it's

00:21:55.540 --> 00:21:58.450
useful to search for a value
in a log without knowing which

00:21:58.450 --> 00:22:00.040
specific field.

00:22:00.040 --> 00:22:02.860
The newly added search
function in BigQuery

00:22:02.860 --> 00:22:05.620
allows you to do just that.

00:22:05.620 --> 00:22:08.890
The function searches across an
entire table or set of columns

00:22:08.890 --> 00:22:10.210
to find a value.

00:22:10.210 --> 00:22:13.150
To demonstrate, let's pick an IP
address and find all the audit

00:22:13.150 --> 00:22:17.360
logs associated with the IP
across any field in the table.

00:22:17.360 --> 00:22:19.660
And we can do that by
using the search function,

00:22:19.660 --> 00:22:24.310
specifying the table, as well
as the IP address defined.

00:22:24.310 --> 00:22:25.990
So by using the
search function I've

00:22:25.990 --> 00:22:29.110
been able to find all of the
records that are associated

00:22:29.110 --> 00:22:32.080
or include this
particular IP address.

00:22:32.080 --> 00:22:35.950
This is extraordinarily useful
for unstructured data that is

00:22:35.950 --> 00:22:39.170
stored in JSON in other fields.

00:22:39.170 --> 00:22:42.350
For example, it's not limited
to IP address or just for audit

00:22:42.350 --> 00:22:42.850
logs.

00:22:42.850 --> 00:22:46.000
It can be used generally
across any of the fields

00:22:46.000 --> 00:22:47.540
within BigQuery.

00:22:47.540 --> 00:22:50.350
Now one of the powerful
features of Log Analytics

00:22:50.350 --> 00:22:52.660
is it also provides you
with access to the same log

00:22:52.660 --> 00:22:53.830
data in BigQuery.

00:22:53.830 --> 00:22:56.680
While Log Analytics provides
an optimized UI and one

00:22:56.680 --> 00:22:58.870
that evolves to add
more optimizations,

00:22:58.870 --> 00:23:00.760
having access to
log data in BigQuery

00:23:00.760 --> 00:23:02.260
means that you can
join the log data

00:23:02.260 --> 00:23:03.970
with other data and BigQuery.

00:23:03.970 --> 00:23:07.780
By clicking the Run in
BigQuery link in Log Analytics,

00:23:07.780 --> 00:23:10.630
the same query is
loaded in BigQuery.

00:23:10.630 --> 00:23:13.480
And running that will
return the same results

00:23:13.480 --> 00:23:16.480
with the standard
BigQuery formatting.

00:23:16.480 --> 00:23:18.710
You'll notice it's
the same data.

00:23:18.710 --> 00:23:22.540
However, the results
appear differently

00:23:22.540 --> 00:23:25.960
for the proto
[INAUDIBLE] format.

00:23:25.960 --> 00:23:30.160
Now in BigQuery, we can
combine that IP address data

00:23:30.160 --> 00:23:32.290
that we looked up in
the previous example

00:23:32.290 --> 00:23:33.820
with other data.

00:23:33.820 --> 00:23:38.930
As an example, I'll combine the
IP address with a public data

00:23:38.930 --> 00:23:41.080
set and use the
functions in BigQuery

00:23:41.080 --> 00:23:43.360
to produce a list
of all the countries

00:23:43.360 --> 00:23:46.450
and the number of logs for each.

00:23:46.450 --> 00:23:50.020
We can do that with this query.

00:23:50.020 --> 00:23:52.660
Here we have the
Select statement,

00:23:52.660 --> 00:23:55.300
which will look at all of the
audit logs over the past 30

00:23:55.300 --> 00:23:58.960
days and select the IP addresses
from them as well as the count.

00:23:58.960 --> 00:24:02.170
And here we'll join that
data with a public data set,

00:24:02.170 --> 00:24:04.720
as well as use the
BigQuery IP functions

00:24:04.720 --> 00:24:06.730
to be able to get the
country associated

00:24:06.730 --> 00:24:07.705
with each of those IPs.

00:24:11.820 --> 00:24:13.980
So by combining
the audit log data

00:24:13.980 --> 00:24:16.128
with the geolocation
public data set,

00:24:16.128 --> 00:24:18.420
I've been able to gain insight
into the requests coming

00:24:18.420 --> 00:24:20.180
from different countries.

00:24:20.180 --> 00:24:22.430
Most of the requests have
come from the United States.

00:24:22.430 --> 00:24:25.140
I do see that other requests
have originated from places

00:24:25.140 --> 00:24:26.850
outside the United States.

00:24:26.850 --> 00:24:31.860
This combination can
provide powerful insights.

00:24:31.860 --> 00:24:34.590
So to recap, Log Analytics
and Cloud Logging

00:24:34.590 --> 00:24:36.660
provides a powerful way
to analyze your logs

00:24:36.660 --> 00:24:38.550
based on the power of BigQuery.

00:24:38.550 --> 00:24:40.860
The Log Analytics UI
provides an optimized way

00:24:40.860 --> 00:24:43.690
to view and work with log
data, and the log data

00:24:43.690 --> 00:24:46.530
stored in Log Analytics is made
available directly in BigQuery

00:24:46.530 --> 00:24:48.870
for joining with other data.

00:24:48.870 --> 00:24:50.820
While we're excited
about Log Analytics,

00:24:50.820 --> 00:24:53.850
we're already hard at work
making it both easier to use

00:24:53.850 --> 00:24:55.525
and more powerful.

00:24:55.525 --> 00:24:57.150
To make it easier to
use, we're working

00:24:57.150 --> 00:25:00.420
on adding a new visualization
result type via charts,

00:25:00.420 --> 00:25:04.260
so that users can more easily
understand their query results.

00:25:04.260 --> 00:25:07.590
We're also working to simplify
the user query experience.

00:25:07.590 --> 00:25:09.930
We know that not everybody
is a SQL expert, which

00:25:09.930 --> 00:25:12.360
is why we're working
to make it easier

00:25:12.360 --> 00:25:14.430
to gain the benefits
of Logging Analytics

00:25:14.430 --> 00:25:17.230
without becoming a SQL expert.

00:25:17.230 --> 00:25:19.940
We're also working to enable
Log Analytics by default.

00:25:19.940 --> 00:25:21.580
This will make it
easier for any users

00:25:21.580 --> 00:25:25.340
to access the advanced
logging capabilities.

00:25:25.340 --> 00:25:27.950
Now to make Log Analytics
an even more powerful tool,

00:25:27.950 --> 00:25:29.763
we're working on
logging insights.

00:25:29.763 --> 00:25:32.180
Logging insights can surface
data that you would otherwise

00:25:32.180 --> 00:25:33.740
have to search to find.

00:25:33.740 --> 00:25:36.020
By analyzing data
proactively, Logging Analytics

00:25:36.020 --> 00:25:38.240
can help uncover
powerful insights,

00:25:38.240 --> 00:25:40.940
such as proactive
recommendations.

00:25:40.940 --> 00:25:43.010
We're also working
to expand the scope

00:25:43.010 --> 00:25:45.380
to include other
operational data.

00:25:45.380 --> 00:25:48.350
By combining logs data with
other operational data,

00:25:48.350 --> 00:25:51.290
such as trace data, you
can gain even more context

00:25:51.290 --> 00:25:53.610
for a more rich analysis.

00:25:53.610 --> 00:25:56.540
We're really excited about
launching the Log Analytics

00:25:56.540 --> 00:26:00.350
feature today, and we're
excited to see the powerful ways

00:26:00.350 --> 00:26:03.910
that you'll use these
new capabilities.

00:26:03.910 --> 00:26:06.690
So on behalf of the whole Cloud
Logging and BigQuery teams,

00:26:06.690 --> 00:26:08.478
thank you for watching.

00:26:08.478 --> 00:26:10.020
To learn more about
Logging Analytics

00:26:10.020 --> 00:26:12.120
and Cloud Logging
please visit our Docs

00:26:12.120 --> 00:26:15.770
and sign up for the
preview to get started.

